WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.582
Deslop looks through the project and brings

00:02.582 --> 00:04.864
possible copies into the sidebar. I open

00:04.864 --> 00:07.146
one of those findings here. Instead of

00:07.146 --> 00:09.428
taking a number on trust, I can

00:09.428 --> 00:11.710
follow its file links and inspect the

00:11.710 --> 00:13.340
exact code that was reported.

00:14.140 --> 00:16.353
These two files use different names, but

00:16.353 --> 00:18.567
the calculation is the same. I open

00:18.567 --> 00:20.780
the comparison to see that directly. Renaming

00:20.780 --> 00:22.993
a class or a variable has not

00:22.993 --> 00:25.207
made the repeated work disappear, and the

00:25.207 --> 00:27.420
side-by-side view makes that easy to check.

00:28.220 --> 00:30.597
I visit each original file and look

00:30.597 --> 00:32.973
beyond the highlighted lines. The surrounding code

00:32.973 --> 00:35.350
tells me how the calculation is used.

00:35.350 --> 00:37.726
This is where I decide whether one

00:37.726 --> 00:40.103
shared helper would make sense, or whether

00:40.103 --> 00:42.140
the two copies should stay separate.

00:42.940 --> 00:45.020
I make an edit in the source

00:45.020 --> 00:47.100
file and save it. Deslop checks the

00:47.100 --> 00:49.180
changed project and refreshes the finding in

00:49.180 --> 00:51.260
the editor. I can compare again straight

00:51.260 --> 00:53.340
away, which keeps the review connected to

00:53.340 --> 00:55.420
the code I am actually working on.
